<section class="p-hero"><div class="p-hero-img"><img src="/assets/img/photos/clipboard-site.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/clipboard-site-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/clipboard-site.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap p-hero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="/appraisal-playbook/">Appraisal playbook</a><span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">California’s Standard Appraisal Clause, Read Line by Line</span></nav><p class="kicker">Guide · 7 min read · November 2, 2026</p><h1>California’s Standard Appraisal Clause, Read Line by Line</h1><p class="lede">What the standard fire policy’s appraisal paragraph actually says about demands, deadlines, umpires and awards, and what it leaves out.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<p>Most California homeowner policies borrow their appraisal language from the standard form fire policy in Insurance Code §2071. Reading it closely answers most of the questions policyholders ask. Here is what it says, in plain English.</p>
<h2>The trigger: failure to agree</h2>
<p>Appraisal is available when the insured and the insurer fail to agree on the actual cash value or the amount of loss. That’s a disagreement about money, not about whether the loss is covered. Either side may start the process with a written request.</p>
<h2>Two appraisers and a 20-day window</h2>
<p>Each side selects a competent and disinterested appraiser and notifies the other within 20 days of the request. Competent means qualified to value the loss. Disinterested means no stake in the outcome beyond a fair fee and no disqualifying relationship with a party.</p>
<h2>The umpire and the 15-day window</h2>
<p>The appraisers first select a competent and disinterested umpire. If they can’t agree within 15 days, either side may ask a judge of a court of record in the state, in the county where the property is located, to choose one.</p>
<h2>Item-by-item valuation</h2>
<p>The appraisers appraise the loss, stating actual cash value and loss for each item separately. Only their differences go to the umpire. That itemization matters: it lets everyone see exactly where the numbers came from, and it lets coverage questions be addressed separately if they exist.</p>
<h2>An informal process</h2>
<p>The standard wording describes appraisal as informal unless both parties agree otherwise, meaning no formal discovery, no formal rules of evidence and no court reporter. The panel works from documents, inspections and the appraisers’ presentations.</p>
<h2>The award and the costs</h2>
<p>A written, itemized award signed by any two of the three, once filed with the insurer, determines the actual cash value and loss. Each side pays its own appraiser; the umpire and other appraisal expenses are split equally.</p>
<h2>What the clause doesn’t do</h2>
<ul><li>It doesn’t decide coverage or causation.</li><li>It doesn’t override policy limits or deductibles.</li><li>It doesn’t, on its own, stop policy deadlines. Ask an attorney about tolling.</li></ul>
<p>Your own policy may add language, such as a requirement to complete appraisal before suing, or details about expenses. Read the appraisal condition in your policy alongside this overview.</p>
